<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Epirus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Epirus is a mountainous region of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindus">Pindus</a> range and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-sea">Ionian Sea</a>, divided today between north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and southern Albania. In antiquity it was home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molossians">Molossians</a> and other Greek-speaking tribes, whose kingdom reached its height under Pyrrhus. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it formed the core of the Despotate of Epirus, and later of Ali Pasha's domain centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/epirus.jpg" alt="Epirus" width="320" height="305" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heinrich Kiepert · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epirus_antiquus_tabula.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
